  "query_preview": "..\nWater vapor imagery shows a ridge of high pressure over the\nintermountain west. The ridge axis is just to our east. PWATS\nare 1.25 inches by Ajo with lower values down to 0.80 inches in\nDouglas. Plus, the upper level flow is southerly to slightly\nsoutheasterly. This sets up the favorite area for any\nthunderstorms west and south of Tucson today because of the\nbetter PWATS values and lots of sunshine to get higher CAPE\nvalues. 12Z HREF has 50-70% chances for thunderstorm wind gusts\ngreater than 35 mph over central Pima County (between Three \nPoints and Ajo). This will bring a risk for blowing dust, \ndepending on how dry the soils tops have been in the last 24 \nhours. Any storm has the ability to produce brief locally heavy\nrainfall, posing a 5% risk for flash flooding. Thunderstorm \nactivity will wane down after sunset.\n\nGoing forward, high pressure will stay around the region for the\nlong haul, prolonging our heat. The ridge axis will slowly move\nwest over the region by Tuesday which will elevate temperatures\nand crank up the HeatRisk across the region to widespread Major\nand Extreme. Cochise and Santa Cruz Counties risk will increase\nto Major by Tuesday and Wednesday. Extreme Heat Warning has \nbeen extended for most of Pima, Pinal, and the valleys of Graham\nand Greenlee through the rest of the new week. Some areas of \nthe warned area will see a brief moment of \"cooling\" (by a 1-3 \ndegrees) on Sunday and Monday while other areas, such as built \nup population areas, will continue to experience Major HeatRisk;\nespecially with the compounding heat over the last days and \nabove normal overnight lows. Any thunderstorms and increase \nthick clouds can reduce or delay those hot temperatures. \n\nDaily slight chances for thunderstorms will continue through \nMonday, mainly favoring west and south of Tucson. Chances will \nreduce each day as the ridge axis moves over the region. Tuesday\nthrough Thursday will be more of the down days during period.\nLong range models indicate an increase trend of thunderstorms by\nthe end of the week due to the favorable upper level flow and\npattern. \n\nIn the extended, CPC outlooks for the 8-14 days has a 60-80%\nlikely above normal temperatures and near normal precipitation\nfor Southeast Arizona.",
